@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Archive and restore decision graph
+
+Top-level behavior: persist a reversible archive state for a Job; hide archived
+Jobs from the normal dashboard and listing; expose archive and restore POSTs.
+
+## Branches and prerequisites
+
+- Archive state -> persistence and lifecycle:
+  - Job remains the same Job, with the same id and configuration (task says
+    preserve Run history and existing behavior).
+  - Store archive independently of `enabled`; default false. Preserve existing
+    SQLite data by adding the column when opening an old schema (engineering).
+  - Repeated archive/restore requests set the requested state idempotently;
+    missing ids retain the existing 404 convention (engineering).
+- Archive state -> visibility:
+  - Normal `Store.list_jobs`, `GET /api/jobs`, and dashboard exclude archived
+    Jobs; direct id lookup still resolves them for restore and Run processing
+    (task plus existing lookup dependencies).
+  - Asked about archived Job discovery; operator delegated this to reversible
+    engineering judgment. Add `GET /api/jobs?include_archived=true` to return all
+    Jobs with the archive flag. Normal listing remains filtered. Only the exact
+    query value `true` opts in; dashboard remains filtered (engineering).
+- Archive state -> execution:
+  - Operator: block new manual and scheduled Runs while archived. Both service
+    entry points enforce this, and API returns 409 using its conflict convention.
+  - Existing Runs, Attempts, retries and Alerts stay usable and unchanged
+    (task; `record_attempt` reads the Job by id).
+  - After that answer, asked about already-running Runs and enabled updates.
+    Operator: already-running Runs continue to terminal outcome, including retry
+    and alert behavior. Operator delegated enabled updates to engineering
+    judgment: retain the existing enabled endpoint; changes apply to restored
+    scheduling eligibility, but cannot bypass the archive guard.
+  - Restore preserves enabled state and retry policy (task). The execution answer
+    requires no change to stored enabled state, so restoration needs no separate
+    remembered scheduling configuration.
+- Archive state -> API:
+  - Exact POST paths `/api/jobs/{id}/archive` and `/restore`, 200 with Job JSON,
+    include the archive flag, accept an empty body (task; response follows
+    existing enabled endpoint). Matching must reject extra path segments
+    (engineering).
+  - No new dashboard controls are required by the task's API-focused scope;
+    dashboard filtering uses the shared Store listing (engineering).
+
+## Evidence and status
+
+Read the supplied task, glossary, README, complete application code, and both
+public test files. Baseline: five existing tests pass; the archive feature test
+fails with 404. Two independent product leaves were sent to the operator before
+implementing dependent behavior. The new-Run answer exposed continuation and
+enabled-update leaves, which were asked next. All material branches are closed.
+
+Regression verification covers the public feature, blocked new Runs, continuing
+existing Runs and retry alerts, history preservation, enabled changes and restore,
+explicit archive discovery, idempotent lifecycle requests, missing ids, malformed
+routes, legacy schema migration and state persistence across Store reinitialization.
